# The build target, lowered from Next 16's default.
#
# With no browserslist config, Next 16 compiles for "baseline widely available" —
# chrome 111, edge 111, firefox 111, safari 16.4 (node_modules/next/dist/shared/
# lib/modern-browserslist-target.js) — and hands that to Turbopack as its
# browserslistQuery. Safari 16.4 happens to be the first version that parses a
# class *static block*, and Next's own App Router client runtime ships one, so the
# emitted chunk threw a SyntaxError during parsing on a phone whose last update was
# iOS 15. Nothing hydrated after it: the page kept its server HTML and lost every
# button that needs JavaScript. Measured on the chunk `/login` loads, taken from the
# running shop: `acorn.parse(code, { ecmaVersion: 2021 })` fails at offset 667 while
# `ecmaVersion: 2022` passes — i.e. an ES2021 engine cannot read it at all.
#
# The floor is 15.6, the newest Safari 15, because the phone is the constraint: an
# iPhone 7 Plus or 6s cannot be updated past iOS 15, and on iOS every browser —
# Safari, Chrome, and the in-app browsers — is WebKit, so there is no browser to
# switch to that escapes it. A shop's customers hold those phones.
#
# Everything else stays exactly where Next put it. `npm run browser:target` fails
# the build if this file disappears or its Safari floor creeps back up, because the
# failure mode above is invisible in every other gate: the markup compiles, the page
# renders, and only a real old phone shows the hole.
chrome 111
edge 111
firefox 111
safari 15.6
ios_saf 15.6
